# Update 55 ceremony log

Upstream `675a0775` (2026-09-02), seed `81F9E817...` (3,179,934 bytes).
Sandbox `20260903T184329Z-u55-driver`. NOT banked; see "What is missing".

What was changed and what was run, in order, one line each. Detail lives in the
commit it points to; findings go to the register, measurements to
JUSTIFICATIONS. This file is the thread, not the record.

## The superset branch

`NewRepository@u56-candidate` (worktree `cobblestone-u56`) is where every fix we
own now lives. It is cut from `u55` and nothing else, so its base is one tag.

Built by CARRYING FORWARD, not by rebasing a chain: cherry-pick what already
applies, and for the zig-emitter split RE-RUN the cut against U55's text, because
U55 edited the very section that moves. Bases were checked with `git merge-base`,
never assumed.

What went in: the memory builtins, the real conversions, the wgsl fixes, the
four-way emitter split, the dead-definition deletion. What did NOT: the f64
bitcasts, which U55 already absorbed.

Tags `u54`, `u55` and `u54-zig-prelude-split` mark where each piece came from.
The per-fix ledger -- branch, base, proof, what it invalidates -- is
`CARRY_FORWARD.md`.

## Our assets that the superset broke

The progression's own prediction, arriving on schedule: a fix is not carried
until every asset that reads the changed thing has been carried too.

- **Three ladder bundlers each held a private `-Chapters @('ZigEmitter',
  'ZigPlug')`.** The four-way split updated the author's build.ps1 and none of
  them. First run: 17 x CDX3002 six seconds in, naming definitions that sat in
  files nobody had asked for. One list now (`zig_plug_pages.txt`), one guard
  (`check_zig_pages.py`, verified RED against a plain U55 tree).

- **The READERS were worse, because they fail silently.** `tier_coverage.py`
  scans for the ZigBuiltinEmitter table, which moved to page 2: it found ZERO
  builtins and would have reported complete coverage of an empty set. Measured,
  0 before and 79 after. `check_builtin_sites.py` found one of its three spans
  and printed a clean sheet for the other two. Neither crashed.

- **`xref bundle` said the split was cite-neutral and it was right about the
  wrong thing.** It was handed a concatenation of the four pages, which is what
  a correct bundle looks like -- so it could not see that the real bundler was
  building a different one. A tool fed the intended input cannot report the
  actual input.

## The ir_to_wire flake, and the day it cost

**ONE failure, ever.** `ir_to_wire`'s bare-metal compile raised `guest stopped
consuming at rpos 1048576 of 1235320` once, 22:42:41, fifteen minutes into the
u56-candidate rebank. Twelve runs since have passed, including three
back-to-back on the same tree with byte-identical input.

**It was not caused by anything we changed.** The commit I spent five box runs
implicating -- the hosted check-compact skip -- had ALREADY passed this rung in
a full rebank on 2026-09-03 00:02 (`20260902T231322Z-u54-hostedkind`, cut from
`f22e9c5f` itself). That evidence was in a run directory the whole time and cost
nothing to read. I bisected instead, and every step of the bisection was noise
read as signal, because the failure is intermittent and I treated n=1 as
deterministic.

Two claims I made and had to withdraw: that the TypeChecker delta was the
trigger, and that this rung had been "dark for two Updates" -- the second taken
from a mid-cycle line in U54.log rather than from the run directories, which
show it passing on 2026-09-01 and three times on 2026-09-02.

**What the guest is actually doing.** `__bare_metal_read_serial` HLTs when
`wpos == rpos` and needs an interrupt to re-check (`hlt` then `jmp wait-loop`,
`X86_64Helpers.codex`). Every subject over 1 MB hits that window once, at the
refill. A healthy run closes it inside one 150 ms poll -- measured `longest
stall 0 rounds` on every pass -- so the wake normally arrives. The failure is a
rare miss of it. The host's claim that "each round's stop/resume doubles as that
missing wake" is doubtful: a gdbstub stop/resume does not inject an interrupt,
and a halted CPU resumes halted.

**Not chased further, deliberately.** n=1, no fix pending, and ten quiet
attempts would not have distinguished a 1-in-50 race from a fixed one. What was
kept instead is the diagnosis: the detector now fails in 3 seconds instead of
60, says WEDGED or BUSY from RIP, names the seed function, and prints the ring
cursors as the GUEST sees them -- so the next occurrence is read off the log
rather than bisected for a day.

## PARKED: ir_to_wire says `error` where the zig arm says `boolean`

The uniform-boxing sweep took the ladder from 6/14 to 11/14. Two rungs remain,
and one of them is a MISMATCH rather than a refusal, which is the failure mode
this whole apparatus exists to find:

    zig  ... (binary le (name "n" int-default) (int-lit 1) boolean) ...
    bare ... (binary le (name "n" int-default) (int-lit 1) error)   ...

BARE METAL produced ErrorTy for a comparison that is plainly boolean, and the
zig arm produced the right answer. That direction is the surprising part and it
is why this is parked rather than dismissed: `error` here is `mcopy-type`'s
unsafe escape, the same signature as the hosted check-compact defect
(COMPILER-43), showing up on the arm that is supposed to be the oracle.

Not chased yet. Before anyone does: the truths in that sweep were carried over
from the u56-rebank2 sandbox rather than re-measured, and the sweep says so
itself ("bare metal was NOT re-measured in this sweep"). So the first question
is whether a fresh bare-metal run still says `error`, and that is one guest, not
a bisection.

The other remaining rung, ir_to_x86, panics with "integer does not fit in
destination type" -- a runtime trap in the emitted zig, also newly reachable now
that boxing lets the program get that far.

## Steps

- **Read before running anything.** 455 files, +51,913/-8,177 over U54. Compiler
  27 files, +1,126/-551: `IR/Lowering` +319, `opening` +133,
  `TypeCheckerInference` +125, `Emit/X86_64Helpers` **-213**.

- **The contracts we hard-code did NOT move**, and U55 touches all three files
  they live in. `serial-ring-buf-addr` 5242880, `serial-ring-buf-size` 1048576,
  `serial-write-pos-addr` 28704, `serial-read-pos-addr` 28712 -- all unchanged,
  matching `ring_compile.py` exactly. `tools/codex-vm.c`'s 121 new lines are a
  Windows WHP teardown race (application processors not stopped before the
  partition was deleted under them); we run QEMU. `build/vm-config.ps1` is host
  memory accounting. `X86_64Boot` changed for two real fixes -- the
  spawn-affinity rest value stored twelve instructions before the page tables
  zeroed its page (COMPILER-49), and capability masks now going through a
  register because `or rax, imm32` sign-extends under REX.W.

- **`seed_identity` does NOT derive Update 55.** `sandbox.sh` reports
  `seed-update unreleased`; U54 derived with no matcher change.

- **U54's seed cannot compile U55's source.** Same blob, two seeds: U55 compiles
  it clean in 5.1 s, U54 halts with eight errors of the form
  `Type mismatch: Con:Integer[T64] vs Integer`. That is the constrained integer
  the trapping work introduced, not a defect -- and it means no cross-seed
  comparison is available on U55 source.

- **Harness gates green at U55 unchanged**: 6 emit roots, 7 bags merged, both
  harnesses gated.

- **The sweep died at rung 7**, `ir_to_codex`, with
  `CDX2001: Type mismatch: Rec:IRChapter vs Fun`. `lower-chapter` takes ELEVEN
  parameters at U55 and answers `(IRChapter, Integer)` -- 8 at U53, 9 at U54.
  Codex curries, so an under-applied call is a VALUE and the error names
  `run-ir-pipeline` rather than the call or the missing argument.

- **The keep protocol is patched in one place** (`ast/emit_harness.py`), because
  `lower_call()` and `LOWER_SETUP` generate all nine call sites. The reservation
  ORDER is the driver's: keep first, then the scratch deck, then
  `__deck-set lower-base`, because `lower-chapter` reads `__deck-pos` as its own
  scratch base and nothing read the position before U55. `lower.ir` rebuilds
  clean, SIZE 6176090.

- **The IR provenance key was blind to the harness that produced it.** `ir_key`
  was (seed, subject bytes, flags), and the subject is the BUNDLER'S OUTPUT --
  produced only after the key says the IR is stale. The rebuild meant to prove
  the keep fix printed nothing and returned success against an IR fifteen
  minutes older than the harness beside it. It now carries `set_hash(unit)`.

## What U55 did with our work

- **Issue 109 -> COMPILER-36 LANDED.** Trapping integer arithmetic: multiply
  21676, add and sub 21798, spelling unit 21902. Plugs row 2.21, zig and csharp
  57 of 57 on the oracle.
- **Issue 115 -> COMPILER-48 instance 5 LANDED** (22074, 22100). The emit deck
  armed a ceiling 27x above its extent with the UD2 unreachable.
- **safari-codex taken in with credit** (22154, 22160). All 27 port chapters
  compile clean, non-vacuously; 730 wasm functions; 18 graders against our golds.
- **COMPILER-42 struck from Damian's queue for good** -- "the agents' problem".
  The ownership-analysis arc landed and the rewrite pass ships OFF by default:
  correctness broken, a self-applied compiler crashes in `mcopy-labels`.
- **PR 116 CLOSED in favour of upstream's smaller cut**, which landed:
  `opening.codex` defines `codex-opening`, `EntryPoint.codex` holds `opening`.
  Their review caught that 18 of our 891 moved lines carried pre-tuple
  `lower-chapter` text, so applying ours would have reverted landed work and
  gated green.
- **Not landed, still ours**: peek-32, poke-32, poke-byte, `__memset`,
  alloc-bytes, real-to-int, real-from-int. Landed: address-of, peek-byte,
  hosted-kind, real-to-bits, bits-to-real.

- **SO THE NATIVE BUILD STILL CANNOT SUCCEED AT PLAIN U55**, for the same reason
  it could not at plain U54, and this is the release fact that matters most to
  us. The compiler's own `Types/Unifier.codex` CALLS `peek-32` in its memo
  slots --

      937:   else let v = peek-32 (slot + 8) 0
      959:   in let k = bit-and (peek-32 slot 0) 4294967295

  -- and U55's `ZigEmitter` has no emitter for it, nor for `poke-32`,
  `__memset` or `alloc-bytes`, all of which the compiler also uses. Our
  `zig-plug-memory-builtins` branch is still the fix and is still unlanded.

  Established by grep in seconds rather than by a thirteen-minute native build,
  which is the whole of "scale the gate to the question": the build would have
  answered the same thing and cost the box.
- **Our plugs rows were renumbered again.** 2.19 and 2.20 now belong to the img
  plug and the evidence plug. A PR that cites a row number cites something that
  moves.

## What the entry-point split bought

`Chapter: Opening` is bundlable, so a harness can CALL the driver instead of
copying it. Proven: the driver compiles inside a rung subject (66,199 lines,
SIZE 14316197), and `zigc`'s harness is now 1,100 bytes where it was ten
thousand -- `compile-frontend-cdx` then `compile-to-cdx`, SIZE 15824631.

Finding the driver's chapter list cost three guest compiles. `xref bundle` now
answers it in four seconds and is wired into `check_bundles.py`.

## What is missing

- **No truth bank for this seed**, so no rung has compared anything. The bank is
  the ceremony's point and it has not been reached.
- **The remaining eight lowering rungs still copy the driver.** `codexir`
  deviates on purpose (`resolve=False`); `lower`, `ir_to_codex` and
  `ir_to_wire` would have to carry the x86 backend to cite it.
- **`opening.codex` reads `run-ir-pipeline` and cites nothing that defines it**
  -- B5's flat namespace hides it upstream. Worth telling Damian.
